  "query_preview": "..\n\nOutside of a very low chance for a stray shower along a convergent \nlake boundary derived from Lake St. Clair/Lake Erie, dry conditions \ncontinue under the influence of a high pressure system that will \nprogress across Ontario. Attention will turn back to active \nconditions across the upper Mid-west/Plains surrounding organization \nand progression of an MCS/MCV. Progression of this system into the \nsouthern Great Lakes/Ohio River valley is expected through the day \ntomorrow, into the evening hours. Specifically for the southern \nGreat Lakes, the thermodynamic environment remains unfavorable for \norganized strong convection. Pre-storm environment highlights \nessentially absent surface-based instability with MUCAPE building to \na few hundred J/kg towards I-94 through the day. The more meaningful \ninstability gradient is expected to develop and hold across central \nIndiana through southern Ohio, which is where the bulk of convective \nactivity is expected to reside. If any developing MCV takes a \nslightly more northern trajectory relative to modeled guidance, this \ncould locally augment ascent and modify the kinematic/thermodynamic \nenvironment and bring stronger storm potential, but current guidance \noffers little indication that these meso processes would be \nsufficient to over the broad scale deficiencies. This scenario is \nhighlighted in the latest SWODY2 where a marginal risk for severe \nweather holds along/south of I-94. \n\nMuch more plausible will be increasing rain shower chances as deep \nmoisture increases ahead of the system. PW values rise aoa 2 inches, \nnear the 99.5th percentile w.r.t August climatology. Aforementioned \nweak instability with poor mid-level lapse rates should limit \nconvective potential, though some isolated embedded thunder and \nlocally heavy rainfall remains possibly given the nature of the \nsubtropical airmass. Rain and thunderstorm chances to continue \nthrough Sunday with better instability in place. Passage of this \nsystem with convectively enhanced low pressure will help to push a \nweak cold front across the region. Rain chances end after passage of \nthe front late Sunday night to Monday morning. Drier conditions are \nfavored through the early week period with highs holding in the low \n80s. \n\n&",
